1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,900
Trapping Rain Water — LeetCode 42

2
00:00:01,900 --> 00:00:05,400
Problem: each number is the height of a wall. After it rains, how much water is trapped between them?

3
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:09,400
Example: height = [2,1,0,3,0,1,2]. Water settles into the dips — six units in total.

4
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:12,600
But why six? Here's the one idea the whole problem rests on.

5
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:17,600
Water above a bar is capped by its shorter wall — the smaller of the tallest walls on each side.

6
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:22,000
Take index 2: its left wall is 2, its right wall is 3. min(2,3) minus its own height 0 = 2 units.

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,600
So we need two numbers per bar: leftMax and rightMax. water[i] = min(leftMax, rightMax) - height.

8
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:31,000
Step one: sweep left to right for leftMax, then right to left for rightMax.

9
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:35,500
Fill each column up to the lower of the two caps, add it up — six units. Correct, but it cost two arrays.

10
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:40,500
Step two: drop the arrays. Two pointers start at the ends and always move the side with the shorter wall.

11
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:45,500
That shorter wall is guaranteed to be the binding cap, so we can bank its water immediately.

12
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:49,500
The pointers march inward, water adds up to the same six — using just two counters.

13
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:53,500
Same answer, no arrays. Time O(n), space O(1).

14
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:58,000
